ern  times had many different styles of  speech and writing okay and what we see  ultimately with the with the advent of a  new modern literature is something like  the vernacular style that is so popular  in Western languages and literatures and  that came about through the medium of  shorthand okay so let's look at some of  the technologies and and you mentioned  you know kind of the opening up of Japan  through Outsiders coming in and and the  thing some of the things that they  brought so let's talk about some of  those things and how big of an influence  was the West on the results that that  you were able to say yeah that's always  the sort of key question and I think  that for a lot of for a lot of scholars  that's been a question that either you  know there was either an endorsement of  the you know Japan was influenced by the  West Japan  you know reproduce those technologies in  its modernization and then there are  sort of others who adopt a kind of anti  colonial or nationalistic perspective  and want to kind of resist the notion  that Japan simply westernized I try to  thread the needle in a little bit of a  different way and so as I really worked  very closely you know again sort of  getting uh you know elbows deep into the  archives what I discovered is that a lot  of the reformers and innovators in Meiji  era Japan we're actually in dialogue  with some of their Western counterparts  so one of the chapters deals with a  fairly obscure figure named asawa Shuji  who went to boston to study and he  actually sought out Alexander Graham  Bell to teach him another one of these  sort of dead india forms called  double speech that Alexander Melville  Belle Graham Bell's father had had  created and he saw I wanted to correct  his pronunciation in Japanese sort of  lose his Japanese accent and the bells  father and son were really involved in  sort of what we would call today like  speech therapy had to help the deaf and  stammerers and the like to correct their  speech I am curious though why did he  want to lose his accent to what purpose  just to be I think understood better  okay but he but he was also an educator  and he was an educator also of the Deaf  and others and I think he was really  interested in this new form of script  that this was during a period in which  is a lot of reform even a generation  earlier people like Benjamin Franklin  had talked about adding and subtracting  letters from the alphabet but visible  speech was a totally different invented  alphabet and I think that it really  appealed to II Sawa in his ideas of how  to transform Japanese language education  but at any rate he learned it directly  from Graham Bell and when Graham Bell  invented the telephone he called asawa  and one of his other Japanese classmates  colleagues to come over to the house as  the story goes and apparently Japanese  was the second language used on the  telephone after English so there are  many such episodes with very high-level  contact between the Japanese reformers  and their their colleagues the first  Minister of Education from Japan  Moriarty was in dialogue with someone  named Whitney who was a thick a Sanskrit  is here at Yale mmm-hmm and a friend of  mine well Fleming is now at Santa  Barbara you know did some very  interesting research on that as well but  in my in my book what I talked about was  the idea that so Mori had this crazy  idea or that's how people often talk  about it of replacing the Japanese  spoken language of the form of  simplified English and everyone laughed  at him and thought he was crazy but what  I discovered is that  Mauri not so much in his direct  correspondence with whitney but looking  back to an earlier generation was  looking back to some of those reformers  who were saying for instance you know in  1789 that the United States should  create its own American English its own  national language on par with its  National Constitution so very often in  Japanese studies these typ